SQL> -- Autonomous Database enables parallel DML by default. Every insert below both reads and writes the
SQL> -- table it targets (the NOT EXISTS guard), and once any serial DML has happened in the transaction a
SQL> -- subsequent parallel one raises ORA-12839. Parallelism is worthless for a dozen seed rows, so turn
SQL> -- it off explicitly rather than relying on the optimizer staying serial -- which is what silently
SQL> -- held for the single-row inserts and broke on the first multi-row one.
